- op: discuss  # b88c
  at: '2026-11-17T14:19:28Z'  # b88c
  by: agent  # b88c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b88c
  clock: 7  # b88c
  nonce: b88c  # b88c
  message: "DIAGNOSED 2026-11-17 14:25 UTC: THE RUNNER CANNOT REACH github.com ON PORT 22. A diagnostic pull request (#2228, closed without merging) ran one step in the per-PR pipeline on the same runner image (python:3.11-bookworm): 'TCP github.com:22 IPv4 140.82.113.4: NOT REACHABLE after 15.0 s (timed out)'; 'TCP github.com:443 IPv4 140.82.113.4: OPEN in 0.0 s'; 'ssh: connect to host github.com port 22: Connection timed out' (OpenSSH 9.2 is in the image; only an IPv4 address resolved). Log: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/fast_smart_test_build_11172026/jomid/probe_step.log. So the publish step's `git ls-remote` over SSH hung until the script's own 120 s limit. The deploy key and the secret are not shown to be wrong: the connection never got as far as a key. THE DECISION IS THE OWNER'S, three ways through: (1) SSH OVER PORT 443, GitHub's ssh.github.com endpoint: the same deploy key, one line in the ssh command, and one host to add to ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md (ssh.github.com is not listed; I did not probe it for that reason, so whether the runner reaches it is NOT KNOWN). My recommendation: no new credential. (2) PUSH OVER HTTPS with a fine-grained token (Contents: write on this repository) as a new secret; github.com:443 is reachable and already allowed. (3) open outbound port 22 on the runner's network; nothing changes in the repository. WHAT I AM DOING WITHOUT A RULING: making the step fail in seconds with ssh's own reason and name the git command that failed (no change of transport). UNTIL ONE OF THE THREE: every scheduled run ends FAIL at publish-verdict although its test steps pass, no verdict reaches origin/badges, and the session-start reader has nothing to read. The stop rule (WI-kidop-falih) and the ledger (WI-taguj-nisos) read published verdicts and wait on this."  # b88c
- op: discuss  # 5d19
